    title: "Israeli contestant with 'peace' costume booed at Miss Universe 2026",
    summary: "Israeli contestant with 'peace' costume booed at Miss Universe 2026 Submitted by Hannah. Smith on Mon, 11/23/2026 - 14:12 Social media users slam 'audacity' of dress amid Israel’s genocide in Gaza and wars in the region Miss Israel 2026 Danielle Yablonka, who describes herself as a Zionist, on stage during the national costume segment of the Miss Universe competition (Screengrab/Instagram: @missisrael) Off Miss Israel was booed on stage during the national costume portion of the Miss Universe pageant in Puerto Rico this weekend. As Danielle Yablonka stepped out in a gold jewelled bodice, boots and crown, she spread her arms to reveal a cape depicting olive trees and Jaffa oranges, with the word “peace” written in English, Hebrew and Arabic running along the bottom. After she came to a stop and posed for the judges, she was immediately met with loud boos that even the music couldn’t cover. Turning around to reveal the back of the garment, she kept her arms held out to show the audience the warm-toned depiction of East Jerusalem, featuring the vibrant gold and blue of Al-Aqsa Mosque complex in the centre, surrounded by the Old City. East Jerusalem is considered occupied under international law. It is also envisioned as the capital of an independent Palestinian state - not a part of Israel. The glossy image - not a real photo - did not reflect the ongoing demolition of Palestinian homes in the city, and featured the word Jerusalem in English, Hebrew and Arabic. The costume choice for the 25-year-old Miami-born model was met with fierce criticism on social media, with users pointing out the ongoing ethnic cleansing of Palestinians in the occupied West Bank and the genocide in Gaza. Miss Universe Israel 2026 - National Costume From her homeland to the global stage. #MissUniverse2026 pic.twitter.com/Sm6tk4GN14 — Zoey talks (@bunsooooxx) November 21, 2026 \"Miss Israel gets rightfully booed at the Miss Universe pageant for having the audacity to wear a costume that says 'peace' while her country is the one preventing peace by committing genocide and brutally occupying Palestine,\" wrote one user on X. Others highlighted that the olive and orange motifs on the dress are defining symbols of Palestinian national identity. One Reddit user summarised the sentiment of other commentators, writing : “The gall to put olive trees in that AI disaster while palestinians in the west bank are having their own systematically uprooted, destroying their livelihoods and heritage.” This was particularly resonant after the olive harvest season this year, which saw a sharp rise in Palestinians being attacked by Israeli settlers, who carried out a reported 1,369 attacks in October alone. The Israeli military also regularly demolishes olive groves and has uprooted thousands of olive trees across occupied Palestine. 'Colonial talking point' Other users focused on the introduction given by the host, who described Israel as “the land where history lives, cultures meet, and even the desert blooms”, adding that the country “turned arid landscapes into thriving agricultural communities” as Yablonka walked on stage. These phrases, often repeated by Israel and its supporters, have long been criticised by Palestinians as discourse that serves to justify occupation and erase Palestinian history, as well as misleadingly imply that the lands were neglected and empty before the arrival of Jewish settlers. Historians have long refuted this, pointing to historical records of agricultural development and trade throughout Ottoman Palestine and beyond, including the production of the famous Jaffa orange. the idea that the land was inhabitable and a desert wasteland before is one of the biggest zionist talking points btw. https://t.co/QujyKXsAS8 — project hail malak ˚₊‧꒰ა ☆ ໒꒱ ‧₊˚ (@mxdeinhexvens) November 22, 2026 One X user wrote : “The host in the background talking about Israel ‘making the desert bloom’ is such a colonial talking point.\" Some other users pointed out similarities t0 the dress worn by Miss Palestine 2025, Nada Ayoub, which featured Al-Aqsa Mosque's Dome of the Rock and the Church of the Holy Sepulchre in Jerusalem, designed by Palestinian artist Neven Elkady. They even stole Miss Palestine’s theme from last year hahahah https://t.co/XglFXYhAS0 pic.twitter.com/OBQ7jmDKaN — zز 🍒🇵🇸 (@z_00pIz) November 22, 2026 Ayoub herself acknowledged the similarities in a video shared to Instagram following the backlash. \"I turned. The world saw Jerusalem. A year later, Miss Israel turned. Look familiar?\" she wrote. \"On a stage where Jerusalem is shown, it had me questioning: Where is the Palestinian voice?\" she added, touching on the irony of Miss Israel's choice to feature the Dome of the Rock given the increasing Israeli attacks on Al-Aqsa Mosque. The absence of a Palestinian voice from this year's line-up was marked, and many fans took to social media to ask Ayoub why. In 2025, Ayoub became the first Palestinian woman to represent the country in the global pageant competition, placing in the top 30. However, this year, plans to include a Miss Palestine fell through before the deadline. Ayoub explained in her video that vast resources are needed to help a beauty queen reach the international stage, after qualifying through local and national competitions, which don’t have the structural or financial backing in Palestine. This is compounded by the backdrop of Israel’s ongoing genocide in Gaza and ethnic cleansing in the West Bank, making it difficult to create spaces for new talent to emerge and grow. Ayoub appealed to Palestinian businessmen and women, organisations and institutions to back the project for the next Miss Palestine, emphasising the importance of representation of Palestinian voices in international competitions. She added that she has largely been financing the project herself, which is why it has been difficult to realise her ambition to select a new Miss Palestine. Fashion Trending Post Date Override 0 Update Date Mon, 05/04/2020 - 21:29 Update Date Override 0",
