    title: "AI spurs funding needs as Jera weighs IPO",
    summary: "Chairman Yukio Kani said power demand globally is growing, while the rapid development of AI is creating a new source of consumption.",
    preview: "Japan’s biggest electricity producer said the artificial intelligence boom is supporting the case for investment in the power sector as it considers ways to raise funds for expansion, including a possible initial public offering. Jera Global Chief Executive Officer and Chairman Yukio Kani said power demand globally is growing, while the rapid development of AI is creating a new source of consumption. That’s increasing the need to secure funds for expansion, he said in an interview in Tokyo. “We require a lot of cash,” Kani said.",
    body: "Japan’s biggest electricity producer said the artificial intelligence boom is supporting the case for investment in the power sector as it considers ways to raise funds for expansion, including a possible initial public offering.\n\nJera Global Chief Executive Officer and Chairman Yukio Kani said power demand globally is growing, while the rapid development of AI is creating a new source of consumption. That’s increasing the need to secure funds for expansion, he said in an interview in Tokyo.\n\n“We require a lot of cash,” Kani said. “We really need to build power stations, strengthen LNG terminals — everything throughout the whole LNG value chain.”\n\nJera, which is jointly owned by utilities Tokyo Electric Power and Chubu Electric Power, has long considered an IPO without ever having announced such a plan. Asked where discussions over a listing currently stand, Kani declined to provide details, saying only that Jera was talking to its shareholders about ways to maximize value.\n\nHe added that the changing global environment presents opportunities for the company, which operates across the value chain from upstream projects and shipping to liquefied natural gas terminals and trading.\n\n“We sit in the middle” of those areas, Kani said. “There’s huge potential out there.”\n\nJera announced last month that it would work with Dell Technologies and U.K. developer RHAELM Holdings to develop AI infrastructure, starting with a 400-megawatt data center near its Chiba thermal power station outside Tokyo.\n\nThe overall project is expected to involve about $15 billion of investment, with Jera focused on supplying power rather than investing directly in the data center.\n\nIn May 2024, the company unveiled a ¥5 trillion ($31 billion) investment plan through 2035 that focuses on three sectors: LNG, renewable energy, and hydrogen and ammonia.\n\nThe subsequent growth of data centers — a “new dimension,” according to Kani — has prompted the company to reassess how it allocates and raises capital.",
